WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.040
The first film read relatedness at the nodes.

2
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.440
This one follows the animals' features.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.500
Sharks and humans both have jaws.

4
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.260
Did they get them from one ancestor, or twice?

5
00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:21.063
A character is a feature that varies among taxa, like jaws.

6
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:25.923
Its forms are its states: jaws present, or jaws absent.

7
00:00:27.243 --> 00:00:29.843
A change of state happens somewhere along a branch.

8
00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:32.547
Which state came first?

9
00:00:33.847 --> 00:00:36.147
Take the outgroup's state as the starting point.

10
00:00:37.587 --> 00:00:40.927
Lamprey has no jaws, so absence is the ancestral state.

11
00:00:42.147 --> 00:00:45.687
That is an assumption, since the outgroup's lineage can change too.

12
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.360
Shark, trout, frog and human all have jaws.

13
00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.220
One gain, on the branch from the root to node J, explains all five tips.

14
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.160
So, on this mapping, jaws in sharks and humans are homologous.

15
00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.540
That means similar because of a single origin in a common ancestor.

16
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.460
Parsimony prefers the mapping that needs the fewest changes.

17
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.160
It is a rule for choosing among explanations.

18
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.540
It does not claim that evolution takes the shortest path.

19
00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:25.483
Now a made-up character, called X. Trout and human have it.

20
00:01:26.763 --> 00:01:28.383
Lamprey, shark and frog do not.

21
00:01:29.663 --> 00:01:31.743
Again, absence is the starting state.

22
00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:34.107
Here is one mapping.

23
00:01:35.627 --> 00:01:39.847
X is gained on the branch into node B, then lost on the branch to frog.

24
00:01:41.087 --> 00:01:44.907
That is one origin, so trout and human share X by homology.

25
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Here is another. X is gained twice, once on the branch to trout and once on the

26
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.440
branch to human. That is convergence.

27
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.920
It means similarity that arose independently in separate lineages.

28
00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:04.170
Which mapping does parsimony prefer?

29
00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.230
Count the marks on each tree in your head.

30
00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.830
What is the fewest changes that could explain X?

31
00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:20.867
Each needs two changes.

32
00:02:22.207 --> 00:02:27.047
No single branch leads to trout and human alone, so one change cannot do it.

33
00:02:28.107 --> 00:02:32.007
A gain on the branch into node J, with two losses, would need three.

34
00:02:33.467 --> 00:02:35.027
So the two mappings tie.

35
00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:39.867
Parsimony alone cannot choose between one origin and two.

36
00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:44.087
Homology or convergence stays an open question.

37
00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:47.667
To break the tie, you need more evidence.

38
00:02:48.987 --> 00:02:51.787
How X develops, or what genes build it.

39
00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:54.347
Fossils with intermediate states.

40
00:02:55.327 --> 00:02:56.887
Or more taxa on the tree.

41
00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.210
So map every change onto a branch.

42
00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:06.550
Two taxa share a state by homology when both inherit it from one gain on a shared

43
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:10.450
branch. Other lineages may later lose it.

44
00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.030
Separate gains are convergence.
